**Sec. 80001**
This section amends the Higher Education Act to exempt specific family-owned assets from the calculation of a student’s expected family contribution for federal financial aid. Effective July 1, 2026, the net value of a family farm on which the family resides, a small business with 100 or fewer full-time equivalent employees owned and controlled by the family, and a commercial fishing business (including vessels and permits) owned and controlled by the family will be excluded. These exemptions apply to award year 2026-2027 and subsequent years.

**Sec. 81001**
This section terminates Federal Direct PLUS loans for graduate and professional students beginning July 1, 2026, and establishes new annual and aggregate loan limits for Federal Direct Unsubsidized Stafford loans. Starting July 1, 2026, graduate students may borrow up to $20,500 annually with a $100,000 aggregate limit, while professional students may borrow up to $50,000 annually with a $200,000 aggregate limit. Parent borrowers are capped at $20,000 annually and $65,000 in aggregate for PLUS loans on behalf of dependent students, and all students face a lifetime aggregate limit of $257,500 for non-PLUS loans. Current students enrolled as of June 30, 2026, may continue under prior limits for up to three years or until program completion, whichever is shorter.

**Sec. 82002**
This section sunsets economic hardship and unemployment deferments for loans made on or after July 1, 2027, and limits forbearance eligibility for such loans to no more than 9 months within any 24-month period. Borrowers receiving loans after this date will no longer be eligible for deferment based on unemployment or economic hardship under specific subsections of the Higher Education Act. These restrictions aim to tighten repayment obligations for newer borrowers.

**Sec. 82003**
This section allows borrowers to rehabilitate defaulted Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) and Direct Loans up to two times, increasing the previous limit of one time, and permits Perkins Loan borrowers to rehabilitate loans twice instead of once. Effective July 1, 2027, borrowers with multiple Direct Loans undergoing rehabilitation must make a total monthly payment of at least $10. These changes apply to loans made, insured, or guaranteed under Title IV of the Higher Education Act.

**Sec. 82004**
This section amends the Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) program to include on-time payments made under the new Repayment Assistance Plan as qualifying payments for loan forgiveness. This ensures that borrowers enrolled in the Repayment Assistance Plan can still qualify for PSLF after 120 qualifying payments. The amendment integrates the new repayment structure with existing forgiveness pathways.

**Sec. 82005**
This section appropriates $1 billion in additional mandatory funds to the Secretary of Education for administrative costs related to servicing Direct Student Loan programs. These funds remain available until expended and are intended to support the operational costs of loan servicing under Parts B and D of the Higher Education Act.

**Sec. 83001**
This section modifies Federal Pell Grant eligibility by including foreign income in the adjusted gross income calculation for dependent and independent students starting July 1, 2026. It also renders students ineligible for Pell Grants if their Student Aid Index equals or exceeds twice the maximum Pell Grant amount for that academic year. These changes aim to refine need-based aid distribution by accounting for international income and high asset levels.

**Sec. 83002**
This section establishes the Workforce Pell Grant program for award year 2026-2027, providing grants to students enrolled in short-term, high-skill workforce programs lasting 150 to 600 clock hours. Eligible programs must be approved by state governors, demonstrate at least 70% completion and job placement rates, and lead to recognized, stackable credentials. Students cannot concurrently receive standard Pell Grants and Workforce Pell Grants, and these grants count toward the 12-semester equivalent duration limit.

**Sec. 83003**
This section increases the authorized appropriation for the Pell Grant shortfall from $2.17 billion to $12.67 billion. This adjustment provides additional funding to cover the gap between appropriated funds and the actual cost of Pell Grants.

**Sec. 83004**
This section makes students ineligible for Federal Pell Grants beginning July 1, 2026, if they receive non-Federal grant aid (from states, institutions, or private sources) equal to or exceeding their cost of attendance. This prevents double-dipping by ensuring Pell Grants are not awarded when other grant aid fully covers educational costs.

**Sec. 84001**
This section denies federal student aid eligibility to educational programs at institutions where the median earnings of graduates (working and not enrolled) fall below the median earnings of comparable working adults for two out of three consecutive years. The comparison uses Census data for adults aged 25-34 with similar educational backgrounds, with specific benchmarks for undergraduate versus graduate programs. Institutions must notify students if a program is at risk, and programs can regain eligibility after two years of ineligibility through an application process.

**Sec. 85001**
This section delays the effectiveness of the 2022 Department of Education regulations on borrower defense to repayment for loans originating before July 1, 2035. It restores the borrower defense regulations that were in effect on July 1, 2020, for these loans. This action effectively reverts to prior standards for evaluating borrower claims of misconduct by educational institutions.

**Sec. 85002**
This section delays the effectiveness of the 2022 Department of Education regulations on closed school discharges for loans originating before July 1, 2035. It restores the closed school discharge regulations that were in effect prior to the 2022 amendments. This ensures that borrowers from closed schools are processed under the previous regulatory framework.

**Sec. 86001**
This section appropriates $40 million to the National Endowment for the Humanities for fiscal year 2025 to procure statues for the National Garden of American Heroes. The funds remain available through fiscal year 2028 and are designated for monuments celebrating American heroes as outlined in specific Executive Orders.

**Sec. 87001**
This section appropriates $300 million to the Office of Refugee Resettlement for fiscal year 2025 to fund enhanced vetting of potential sponsors for unaccompanied alien children. Funds cover background checks, home studies, gang-related tattoo examinations, data system improvements, and coordination with state child welfare agencies. The vetting process includes criminal history checks, interviews, and suitability studies for sponsors and all adult household residents. [end of text]


